            <div class="lesson-body">
                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Dealing with Errors Gracefully</h2>
                    <p>Things go wrong. Users enter invalid data, files don't exist, network connections fail. <strong>Exception handling</strong> allows your program to respond to errors gracefully instead of crashing.</p>
                    
                    <p>In this lesson, you'll learn how to catch, handle, and throw exceptions, making your applications more robust and user-friendly.</p>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>What is an Exception?</h2>
                    <p>An <strong>exception</strong> is an object that represents an error or unexpected situation. When something goes wrong, .NET "throws" an exception, which disrupts normal program flow.</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// Without exception handling, errors crash the program
int[] numbers = { 1, 2, 3 };
Console.WriteLine(numbers[10]);  // IndexOutOfRangeException - CRASH!

int result = 10 / 0;  // DivideByZeroException - CRASH!

string text = null;
Console.WriteLine(text.Length);  // NullReferenceException - CRASH!</code></pre>

                    <div class="exception-flow">
                        <div class="flow-step error">
                            <span class="step-icon">⚠️</span>
                            <span>Error Occurs</span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
                        <div class="flow-step exception">
                            <span class="step-icon">📦</span>
                            <span>Exception Thrown</span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
                        <div class="flow-step handle">
                            <span class="step-icon">🛡️</span>
                            <span>Caught &amp; Handled</span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
                        <div class="flow-step success">
                            <span class="step-icon">✅</span>
                            <span>Program Continues</span>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Try-Catch Blocks</h2>
                    <p>Use <code>try-catch</code> to handle exceptions:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">try
{
    // Code that might throw an exception
    int[] numbers = { 1, 2, 3 };
    Console.WriteLine(numbers[10]);  // This throws!
}
catch (IndexOutOfRangeException ex)
{
    // Handle the specific exception
    Console.WriteLine($"Error: {ex.Message}");
}

Console.WriteLine("Program continues...");  // This still runs!</code></pre>

                    <h3>Catching Multiple Exception Types</h3>
                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">try
{
    Console.Write("Enter a number: ");
    string input = Console.ReadLine();
    int number = int.Parse(input);
    int result = 100 / number;
    Console.WriteLine($"Result: {result}");
}
catch (FormatException)
{
    Console.WriteLine("That's not a valid number!");
}
catch (DivideByZeroException)
{
    Console.WriteLine("Cannot divide by zero!");
}
catch (Exception ex)  // Catches any other exception
{
    Console.WriteLine($"Unexpected error: {ex.Message}");
}</code></pre>

                    <div class="info-box warning">
                        <div class="info-box-header">
                            <svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
                                <path d="M10.29 3.86L1.82 18a2 2 0 0 0 1.71 3h16.94a2 2 0 0 0 1.71-3L13.71 3.86a2 2 0 0 0-3.42 0z"/>
                                <line x1="12" y1="9" x2="12" y2="13"/>
                                <line x1="12" y1="17" x2="12.01" y2="17"/>
                            </svg>
                            <span>Order Matters!</span>
                        </div>
                        <p>Catch more specific exceptions first. The base <code>Exception</code> class catches everything, so put it last.</p>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>The Exception Object</h2>
                    <p>The caught exception object contains useful information:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">try
{
    int[] arr = { 1, 2, 3 };
    Console.WriteLine(arr[100]);
}
catch (Exception ex)
{
    // Common exception properties
    Console.WriteLine($"Message: {ex.Message}");
    Console.WriteLine($"Type: {ex.GetType().Name}");
    Console.WriteLine($"Source: {ex.Source}");
    Console.WriteLine($"Stack Trace: {ex.StackTrace}");
    
    // Inner exception (if this was caused by another exception)
    if (ex.InnerException != null)
    {
        Console.WriteLine($"Inner: {ex.InnerException.Message}");
    }
}</code></pre>

                    <div class="property-table">
                        <div class="property-row header">
                            <span>Property</span>
                            <span>Description</span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="property-row">
                            <code>Message</code>
                            <span>Human-readable error description</span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="property-row">
                            <code>StackTrace</code>
                            <span>Call stack showing where error occurred</span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="property-row">
                            <code>Source</code>
                            <span>Name of the application/assembly</span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="property-row">
                            <code>InnerException</code>
                            <span>The exception that caused this one</span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="property-row">
                            <code>Data</code>
                            <span>Dictionary for additional information</span>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>The finally Block</h2>
                    <p>The <code>finally</code> block always executes, whether an exception occurred or not. Use it for cleanup:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">StreamReader? reader = null;
try
{
    reader = new StreamReader("data.txt");
    string content = reader.ReadToEnd();
    Console.WriteLine(content);
}
catch (FileNotFoundException)
{
    Console.WriteLine("File not found!");
}
catch (IOException ex)
{
    Console.WriteLine($"IO Error: {ex.Message}");
}
finally
{
    // Always runs - perfect for cleanup
    reader?.Dispose();
    Console.WriteLine("Cleanup complete.");
}</code></pre>

                    <div class="try-catch-diagram">
                        <div class="diagram-block try">
                            <div class="block-header">try</div>
                            <div class="block-content">Code that might fail</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="diagram-block catch">
                            <div class="block-header">catch</div>
                            <div class="block-content">Handle the error</div>
                            <div class="block-note">(only if exception)</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="diagram-block finally">
                            <div class="block-header">finally</div>
                            <div class="block-content">Cleanup code</div>
                            <div class="block-note">(always runs)</div>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <h3>Using Statement (Better for Cleanup)</h3>
                    <p>For objects that implement <code>IDisposable</code>, use the <code>using</code> statement instead:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// The 'using' statement automatically disposes the resource
using (StreamReader reader = new StreamReader("data.txt"))
{
    string content = reader.ReadToEnd();
    Console.WriteLine(content);
}  // reader.Dispose() called automatically, even if exception!

// Modern C# - using declaration (no braces needed)
using StreamReader reader = new StreamReader("data.txt");
string content = reader.ReadToEnd();
// Disposed when variable goes out of scope</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Throwing Exceptions</h2>
                    <p>You can throw exceptions to signal errors in your own code:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">void SetAge(int age)
{
    if (age &lt; 0)
    {
        throw new ArgumentException("Age cannot be negative", nameof(age));
    }
    if (age &gt; 150)
    {
        throw new ArgumentOutOfRangeException(nameof(age), "Age seems unrealistic");
    }
    
    // Valid age, continue...
}

// Usage
try
{
    SetAge(-5);
}
catch (ArgumentException ex)
{
    Console.WriteLine(ex.Message);  // Age cannot be negative (Parameter 'age')
}</code></pre>

                    <h3>Re-throwing Exceptions</h3>
                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">try
{
    DoSomethingRisky();
}
catch (Exception ex)
{
    // Log the error
    Console.WriteLine($"Error logged: {ex.Message}");
    
    // Re-throw to let caller handle it too
    throw;  // Preserves original stack trace
    
    // DON'T do this (loses stack trace):
    // throw ex;
}</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Common Exception Types</h2>

                    <div class="exception-cards">
